## Analysis toolkit — `{{ profiler_mcp_name }}` MCP tools OR shell

If the `{{ profiler_mcp_name }}` MCP server is attached to this round, call its tools directly. Otherwise, shell out to the same subcommand on `python {{ profiler_support_name }}/analyze_torch_profile.py`.

| MCP tool | Shell equivalent | Purpose |
| -------- | ---------------- | ------- |
| `certify(trace)` | `... certify <trace>` | **Run this first** on a raw `*.pt.trace.json(.gz)` trace. PASS/WARN/FAIL on kernel/op counts, `record_shapes` coverage, step markers, GPU-busy fraction, and graph-replay attribution: each failure names the exact re-capture fix. |
| `tables(report)` | `python {{ profiler_support_name }}/analyze_torch_profile.py tables <report>` | Overview: event categories + totals. |
| `kernels(report, top=15)` | `... kernels <report> --top 15` | Top GPU kernels by self-CUDA time. |
| `operators(report, top=15)` | `... operators <report> --top 15` | Top operators (aten::*, torch::*) by self-CPU time. |
| `cpu_overhead(report)` | `... cpu-overhead <report>` | CPU-vs-GPU ratio — detects launch-bound / compute-bound. |
| `memory(report)` | `... memory <report>` | Memory allocation / transfer events. |
| `gemm_shapes(trace, top=20, out=None)` | `... gemm-shapes <trace> --top 20 [--out shapes.json]` | Ranked (M, N, K, dtype) GEMM demand from `aten::mm`/`addmm`/`bmm`/`baddbmm`/`linear`/`matmul`/`_scaled_mm`, weighted by call count and correlated GPU kernel time. `--out` writes JSON usable as GEMM-tuning input (e.g. hipBLASLt/AITER). |
| `roofline(trace, device=None, peak_tflops=None, peak_gbps=None, top=20)` | `... roofline <trace> [--device mi210\|mi300x\|mi300a\|mi325x\|mi355x\|h100] [--peak-tflops X --peak-gbps Y]` | Achieved TFLOP/s, GB/s, arithmetic intensity, and compute-vs-memory bound per GEMM/attention op. Device peaks auto-detect from the trace when omitted. |
| `summary(report, top=15)` | `... summary <report>` | All-in-one: certify (on a raw trace) + overhead + kernels + operators + memory. |

`kernels`/`operators`/`cpu_overhead`/`memory`/`tables`/`summary` accept either
a raw `*.pt.trace.json(.gz)` trace or the JSON `capture` writes: the raw
trace is converted in-process. `certify`/`gemm_shapes`/`roofline` need the raw
trace itself: shape and kernel-correlation detail a summarized report does
not carry.

**Certify before analyzing.** Run `certify` on any raw trace before trusting
`gemm_shapes`, `roofline`, or a per-op reading from `kernels`/`operators`. A
FAIL means the trace cannot support the claim you are about to make; a WARN
means the number is usable but degraded (e.g. per-op attribution is unreliable
under a graph replay). Re-capture using the fix each item names rather than
reporting a number the trace cannot support.

Start with `tables`, then `certify`, then pick the analyses that matter most
for the current architecture.

Suggestions are advisory. Report visibility limits rather than declaring the
optimization loop blocked on new profiler infrastructure. When capture is
unavailable, name the smallest missing capability and any direct end-to-end A/B
that could still discriminate candidate mechanisms.

## Capturing a profile

Capture is the long-running step — run it first, then call the MCP tools on the resulting JSON.

{% if profile_execution == "remote" %}
### Remote capture (REQUIRED on this run)

The editor environment is not the authoritative accelerator runtime. Do not run
`analyze_torch_profile.py capture` locally when that would miss the production
hot path. Use the environment- and domain-provided remote profiling workflow.
Falling back to synthetic data, an unrelated workload, or a placeholder summary
is a profiler failure.

If no remote profiling command is available, report that profiling could not be
captured and explain which entry point or command the implementer must add.

If a remote profiling command fails (entrypoint missing, function errors, JSON empty, returned dict missing required keys, etc.), record the exact error in `analysis` and surface the missing contract as a `suggestions` item. A profile run that produces fabricated or unrelated numbers is worse than a profile run that admits it could not capture.
{% else %}
### Mode A: In-process (default)

Loads the candidate implementation directly and profiles the domain's hot path in a loop. Captures kernel-level detail but may not cover service, batching, or queueing overhead.

```
python {{ profiler_support_name }}/analyze_torch_profile.py capture \
  <domain-specific capture args> \
  --output /tmp/prof.json
```

Use the exact capture command above only when it matches the domain's artifact contract. Otherwise, adapt the capture workflow to the domain context and benchmark harness.

### Mode B: Server-path (optional)

Include HTTP, async, and batching overhead by instrumenting the server with two admin endpoints and using `capture-server`. See the docstring of `analyze_torch_profile.py` for the required `/admin/profile/start` and `/admin/profile/stop` contract.

### Mode C: the candidate's own torch.profiler wiring (if it exposes one)

If the candidate is vLLM (or another server that wires up `torch.profiler`
the same way), check what the **installed** version actually exposes before
assuming a fixed contract: this has changed across vLLM releases, and an
env-var/endpoint contract that worked on one version can silently stop
working on another (e.g. `VLLM_TORCH_PROFILER_DIR` not wired to any
`vllm serve` flag, `POST /start_profile` 404ing unconditionally). Inspect the
installed build's profiler config surface directly, e.g.:

```
python3 -c "from vllm.config.profiler import ProfilerConfig; import inspect; print(inspect.signature(ProfilerConfig))"
```

The most reliable capture path when the objective concerns vLLM's own
request path is the **in-process engine**, one trace per run: construct
`vllm.LLM(...)` with a `profiler_config` kwarg (field names per the installed
version's `ProfilerConfig`, typically including `profiler`,
`torch_profiler_dir`, `torch_profiler_record_shapes`), run an untraced
warmup (compiles kernels / captures HIP graphs so they don't pollute the
profiled window), then bracket only the traced request(s) with
`start_profile()`/`stop_profile()`:

```python
llm = LLM(model=..., profiler_config={
    "profiler": "torch",
    "torch_profiler_dir": "<out_dir>",
    "torch_profiler_record_shapes": True,  # required, see note below
})
llm.generate(warmup_prompts, warmup_sampling)   # untraced warmup
llm.start_profile()
llm.generate(prompts, sampling)                 # the traced request(s)
llm.stop_profile()
```

This writes a raw `*.pt.trace.json(.gz)`, the same format every tool above
accepts. Prefer it over Mode A/B for the server's own request path: it
exercises the real engine (batching, admission, scheduling) rather than a
hand-rolled harness or a server-mode admin-endpoint contract the installed
build may not have. **Run `certify` on the resulting trace before anything
else** (see "Certify before analyzing" above): this is where a missing
`record_shapes` or an unexpectedly graph-mode capture shows up.

Pass `record_shapes=True` (Mode A: to `torch.profiler.profile(...)` directly; Mode C: `torch_profiler_record_shapes` in `profiler_config`). Without it, `certify` will FAIL and `gemm_shapes`/`roofline` have no shapes to extract.

**Post-capture hang (ROCm).** `stop_profile()` (or exiting the `torch.profiler`
context) can hang for several minutes with no further log output (reproduced
on both ROCm 6.4/torch 2.9.1 and ROCm 7.2.3/torch 2.12, so treat it as a
standing ROCm risk rather than a version-pinned bug). This is a
process-teardown hang, **not** a data-loss issue: the trace file is complete
and valid on disk the moment its size/mtime stops advancing, well before the
process would otherwise exit. Wrap the whole capture invocation in an outer
`timeout -k 10 <seconds> ...`, and treat a `124`/`137` exit code as
success-with-a-known-hang rather than a failure; check the output directory
for the trace file regardless of exit code, and do not block indefinitely or
leave a stuck GPU for the next round.
